It is a pun. Also a very old British pun, based I believe on a type of
upperclass accent which does indeed pronounce "surely", the word, and
"Shirley", the name (at the time, more often male than female), in a
remarkably similar manner.

"Surely you can't be serious"
"I am serious and don't call me Shirley"

I have a suspicion that this joke was also used in the popular TV
programme "Dad's Army", where Shirley is in fact the little-used
first-name of Captain Mainwaring (which by the way is for some reason
pronounced "Mannering").

(Sergeant Wilson: "Surely you can't be serious, sir?"
Captain Mainwaring: "I am serious and don't call me..." (*stops,
realises what he's about to say*) "...oh, forget it")

Admittedly I've never actually heard it in the programme, but if it
wasn't used at all then I, for one, will be very surprised, given the
number of other very British in-jokes it contained...

